Michael Roberts, who's with uh Laser FX International.

43
00:03:27,560 --> 00:03:29,160
Michael, welcome to the show.

44
00:03:29,160 --> 00:03:30,440
Well, thank you very much.

45
00:03:30,440 --> 00:03:36,200
We're going to get some information from him on a uh convention that's coming up, among other things that we're going to speak about tonight.

46
00:03:36,200 --> 00:03:41,880
But why don't we uh go right in with the Laser FX ninety-eight convention that's happening May 16th to 18th?

47
00:03:41,880 --> 00:03:43,000
Can you tell us a little about it?

48
00:03:43,320 --> 00:03:43,640
Sure.

49
00:03:43,640 --> 00:03:47,040
This is our uh fourth annual uh Laser FX event.

50
00:03:44,920 --> 00:03:49,760
Uh we hold them in Burlington, Ontario.

51
00:03:50,080 --> 00:03:59,200
Uh part of the reason for that is that uh audience scanning, European style audience scanning laser shows are legal in Canada and audience scanning is not legal in the US.

52
00:03:59,200 --> 00:04:00,240
Oh, I see.

53
00:04:00,240 --> 00:04:03,520
The uh conference is a uh three-day event.

54
00:04:03,520 --> 00:04:07,920
On uh Saturday we have uh a number of uh uh committee meetings.

55
00:04:07,920 --> 00:04:12,640
The International Laser Display Association will be holding their technical committee meeting.

56
00:04:12,640 --> 00:04:14,400
There'll be other groups meeting.

57
00:04:14,400 --> 00:04:21,040
We have an open house on Saturday night, Sunday is a trade show most of the day, Sunday evening is the uh big event.

58
00:04:21,040 --> 00:04:24,080
Uh it's uh the Brewster Awards banquet.

59
00:04:24,080 --> 00:04:30,800
We have a sumptuous buffet dinner, and then everyone sits down to look at laser shows, which are played back from A.

60
00:04:31,040 --> 00:04:37,440
Tape using a full color uh laser system with fiber optic remote uh beam heads.

61
00:04:37,440 --> 00:04:49,600
And everyone who's in the room gets a ballot and gets to vote on what they consider to be the best laser show that they saw that evening, and the winner is awarded with fame and lovely lovely uh award to put on their shelf.

62
00:04:49,600 --> 00:04:51,440
Are the winners awarded that night?

63
00:04:51,440 --> 00:04:59,760
Uh well yes, we announce the winners immediately, but obviously we can't give out the awards immediately because we don't uh ability to engrave them in first thing.

64
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:02,880
With all those lasers, you can't do any laser engraving on the spot, huh?

65
00:05:03,600 --> 00:05:08,320
No, the lasers are all visible, and typically for laser engraving you want to use CO2 lasers.

66
00:05:08,480 --> 00:05:09,360
Yeah, infrared.

67
00:05:09,360 --> 00:05:16,080
Then the Monday is the seminar day, and uh on that day we have a number of uh seminars on various topics.

68
00:05:16,080 --> 00:05:21,040
Uh we have a business issues uh round table with a number of successful laserists.

69
00:05:21,040 --> 00:05:32,200
We have uh what's new technical session in which people with new goods and equipment will discuss the technical workings of uh how they operate and what makes them better than other products.

70
00:05:29,440 --> 00:05:34,680
Could you explain to the listeners and myself as well.

71
00:05:29,520 --> 00:05:37,400
I'm not sure what audience scanning means.

72
00:05:37,400 --> 00:05:38,360
What is that?

73
00:05:38,360 --> 00:05:44,600
Uh audience scanning is where the laser beam is projected directly onto the people in the audience.

74
00:05:44,600 --> 00:05:47,960
Oh sort of like a spread of light that goes up and down or sideways or something?

75
00:05:48,200 --> 00:05:48,600
Correct.

76
00:05:48,600 --> 00:05:51,560
Well there are many different forms of audience scanning effects.

77
00:05:51,560 --> 00:05:54,680
There's sheets, there's waves, there's uh fans.

78
00:05:55,480 --> 00:06:01,880
With fashion shows and things like uh the models walk down through the uh through tunnels of cones of light, yes.

79
00:06:01,880 --> 00:06:04,760
Typically you see this mostly outside the USA.

80
00:06:04,760 --> 00:06:10,920
It's not seen in the USA at all except perhaps it's some underground and illegal raves using low power lasers.

81
00:06:10,920 --> 00:06:22,840
Yeah because it is illegal to scan onto the well it's not illegal, it's just technically extremely complex, requires a lot of safeguards and a lot of paperwork in order to scan onto the audience in the United States.

82
00:06:22,840 --> 00:06:29,640
But in Europe there are companies that have laser systems as powerful as fifty seven watts that scan directly onto the audience.

83
00:06:29,960 --> 00:06:33,160
Now obviously the audience is not standing a few feet away.

84
00:06:33,480 --> 00:06:37,640
They're usually on the other side of a lake or uh in a large public area.

85
00:06:37,640 --> 00:06:40,520
So the what we have to look at is energy density.

86
00:06:40,520 --> 00:07:00,520
Now one of the things we're doing this year because there's been a certain amount of controversy about uh audience scanning is having a special audience scanning workshop at the uh uh sem on seminar day at Laser FX and that is uh to demonstrate to people what is the a good way and a bad way to do things.

87
00:07:00,520 --> 00:07:15,520
For example, if you were projecting a square tunnel, you would want to be sure that the corners were not any brighter than any other part because that poses a hazard in terms of of the energy because there's more energy then in the corners than there is in the rest of the beam.

88
00:07:14,840 --> 00:07:17,600
Sometimes you see actual dots in the corner.

89
00:07:17,600 --> 00:07:20,160
That's because it's being scanned more than once in that area, is that correct?

90
00:07:20,320 --> 00:07:20,720
That's right.

91
00:07:20,720 --> 00:07:31,280
That's because usually there are a number of points in that corner that uh that are there designed to uh hold the squareness of the beam in place as it changes direction, often called anchor points.

92
00:07:31,680 --> 00:07:40,640
From what I understand, uh there's a hot topic in Las Vegas right now with uh you well, actually between the laser light show people and the airport authorities.

93
00:07:40,640 --> 00:07:42,080
Uh are you aware of this?

94
00:07:42,080 --> 00:07:49,520
Oh yes, this has been a very hot issue in the industry and continues to be a hot issue in the industry, lasers in uh airspace.

95
00:07:49,520 --> 00:08:00,400
How this came about is that uh a number of Las Vegas hotels installed very large uh laser shows to shoot beams of light into the sky as part of uh attracting people to their facilities.

96
00:08:01,360 --> 00:08:10,720
And in part this used a uh new technology called the YAG laser, which is an infrared laser that's been frequency doubled to 532 nanometers.

97
00:08:11,200 --> 00:08:17,200
The 532 spectral line is very, very close to the peak sensitivity of the human eye.

98
00:08:17,200 --> 00:08:24,640
So for example, a 10 watt Jag laser would appear as bright to the human eye as a 30 or 35 watt argon laser.

99
00:08:25,200 --> 00:08:40,240
So some of these places installed 30 and 40 watt and some even brighter than that and multiple uh of these lasers and shot them out into the uh night sky, and they did it all in compliance with existing regulations.

100
00:08:40,240 --> 00:08:49,120
But a number of uh pilots got flashed as they were coming into land, and there's a perception amongst a lot of people that lasers are sort of like death rays.

101
00:08:49,120 --> 00:08:49,600
Yes.

102
00:08:49,600 --> 00:08:56,320
And that if you touch it, your face will melt like the guy in uh in uh Raiders of the Lost Arc.

103
00:08:56,320 --> 00:09:01,400
But in actual fact, the real danger is that you get momentary flash blindness.

104
00:09:01,560 --> 00:09:12,440
In other words, it's very similar to the effect of having your photograph taken at a dimly lit party where you sort of see that after image and you're a little startled, uh just the flash of light.

105
00:09:12,520 --> 00:09:13,960
You see that floater.

106
00:09:13,960 --> 00:09:14,600
Mm-hmm.

107
00:09:14,920 --> 00:09:24,280
And so obviously, pilots who are in critical phases of flight, such as takeoff and landing, meet absolutely minimal distractions.

108
00:09:24,280 --> 00:09:37,960
Uh and there have been some incidents where the pilots them s themselves, even when they're not in critical phases, have passed through or flown into a laser beam uh or been scanned by it momentarily and have reacted uh poorly.

109
00:09:37,960 --> 00:09:59,880
In other words, far that'll say to the co-pilot, hey look over there, there's a laser, and they both look in that direction, and of course are momentarily flash blinded and disoriented, and there's been instances where pilots have rubbed their eyes uh very severely and then gone and claimed that they were damaged by the laser, and in actual fact there was more damage from rubbing their eyes than if they just left them alone.

110
00:09:59,880 --> 00:10:14,120
So the uh International Laser Display Association in the States got together uh with uh the SAG G10 people who are uh technical group responsible for advising on rules and regulations and worked with the C D R H in the States.

111
00:10:14,120 --> 00:10:32,240
There's a similar group of laserists here in Canada who are currently working with the uh Canadian government to uh improve the rules and the way things are done and to make these shows safer by doing things like having sparters, in some cases a couple of the hotels in Las Vegas installed radar systems.

112
00:10:32,400 --> 00:10:38,680
So that if the uh be uh an aircraft should transit the beam, the beam could be turned off.

113
00:10:38,680 --> 00:10:47,200
So these uh kinds of uh measures have been taken to increase the safety, but the biggest thing that's not being done so far is pilot education.

114
00:10:44,680 --> 00:10:57,520
In other words, pilots have hundreds of hours of simulator training to handle every conceivable kind of emergency, but they're not trained how to deal with uh a bright flash of light.

115
00:10:57,520 --> 00:10:58,320
Mm-hmm.

116
00:10:58,320 --> 00:11:14,160
So that is that is the remaining area that needs to be worked on uh so far we've been very fortunate that there have been no serious incidents uh in this regard but the industry overall is very cognizant of uh of that kind of thing and is taking measures to prevent anything from happening.

117
00:11:14,160 --> 00:11:19,920
Do you need a license to do any laser light show or does that license kick in the certain power?

118
00:11:19,920 --> 00:11:31,440
Uh uh first of all it's country specific in other words this varies from country to country in certain South American countries the authorities go laser what's that.

119
00:11:31,440 --> 00:11:40,240
In other countries such as the United States where your government is very fond of extreme bureaucracy and large piles of paperwork, that's indeed what you face.

120
00:11:40,640 --> 00:11:51,200
To make a general rule for uh the United States uh anything under four point nine milliwatts is quite sa is considered safe and doesn't require any paperwork.

121
00:11:51,200 --> 00:12:02,720
But as soon as you use a five milliwatt or greater laser in public, then you're required to have a variance for the equipment which is something you have to get from the C D R H division of the FDA.

122
00:12:02,720 --> 00:12:13,520
Now if someone were to buy some laser equipment they buy a five milliwatt laser, they buy a little beam scan unit and they want to go out and they want to do different parties and things with that.

123
00:12:13,520 --> 00:12:24,840
Do they need a license and and and to feel paperwork for each one of those jobs or just yes, you need you need a site variance for each place that you do a laser show, plus you need a variance for the equipment.

124
00:12:24,720 --> 00:12:25,160
Mm-hmm.

125
00:12:26,160 --> 00:12:28,760
What's happening with lasers in some of the other fields?

126
00:12:28,760 --> 00:12:34,680
Well, actually, believe it or not, the hottest topic uh that that's on the horizon is laser pointers.

127
00:12:29,760 --> 00:12:40,920
Uh the UK recently banned outright the sale of laser pointers over power.

128
00:12:41,240 --> 00:12:41,880
Yes.

129
00:12:41,880 --> 00:12:47,080
Uh France is apparently looking into doing the same thing, and there's been a lot of concern about this now.

130
00:12:47,080 --> 00:12:54,440
Back in the old days when someone wanted to buy a laser, they would be looking at getting a helium neon laser which requires a power cord.

131
00:12:54,840 --> 00:13:03,480
Uh it cost a hundred or two hundred dollars and you know, is a twelve or eighteen inch long device, and so therefore they weren't particularly portable.

132
00:13:03,480 --> 00:13:22,120
But now we live in an age where we have these little laser diode pens that you can buy at Business Depot and Radio Shack, and uh they put out about five uh milliwatts of power and people are using them at hockey games and soccer games and concerts and so on, and they're becoming a downright annoyance.

133
00:13:22,120 --> 00:13:22,680
Mm-hmm.

134
00:13:22,680 --> 00:13:38,840
Now the the most sensitive part of the human body to the laser is the eye, and just as you can use a magnifying glass to focus the sun and burn something with it, the lens in the human eye also focuses a laser beam into a very fine point on the back of the eye.

135
00:13:38,840 --> 00:13:53,800
So for example, one milliwatt going in based on the focusing effect of the eye could produce an energy density uh close to ten thousand milliwatts on the surface of the retina and could burn the retina.

136
00:13:53,800 --> 00:13:56,360
Now there are a lot of factors that enter into this.

137
00:13:56,360 --> 00:14:03,160
There's the blink aversion response, which means when a bright light hits your eye, you blink and you look away.

138
00:14:03,160 --> 00:14:06,760
Uh the human eye doesn't actually ever stand still.

139
00:14:06,760 --> 00:14:08,040
It sort of dizes.

140
00:14:08,040 --> 00:14:16,000
Uh even when you're looking at something, it moves minutely up and down at all times to keep the image moving on the rods and cones at the back of the eye.

141
00:14:14,680 --> 00:14:29,600
So in and of themselves the laser pointer is not likely to cause any eye damage, particularly when you've got a situation where there's a person holding it in their hand and another person several feet away, these are two human beings.

142
00:14:29,600 --> 00:14:33,280
Human beings are not rigidly fixed in space like buildings.

143
00:14:33,280 --> 00:14:35,520
But it's an annoyance.

144
00:14:35,520 --> 00:15:03,920
Uh it's rude and it's very distracting and you can have uh uh situations where people get distracted at critical times or if you have uh say uh a sports event and there have been reports of this happening where there are fifty or sixty people in the crowd all pointing laser pointers at the same place, it becomes very annoying and difficult for whomever those pointers are being pointed at to get on with whatever they're supposed to be doing.

145
00:15:04,320 --> 00:15:19,680
So I suspect that we will probably see more rigid enforcement in uh in North America of uh sales of laser pointers and I think that it behooves people who use them to be responsible in what they do and not point them in anyone's face.
